  "names": {
    "order": "given family",
    "conventions": "Mauritius is a mix of communities and names show it. Hindu Mauritians, mostly descended from Bhojpuri-speaking indentured workers, carry Indian first names with surnames spelled the colonial way (Beeharry, Seechurn, Ramlogun). Tamil and Telugu families have their own surnames (Pillay, Moorghen, Veerasamy, Appadoo). Muslim Mauritians carry Arabic and Indian Muslim names, again with Mauritian spellings (Shafick, Jaufeerally). Creoles mostly have French first names and French surnames, often double first names like Jean-Marc or Marie-Claire. Sino-Mauritians usually have a French or English first name with a Hakka surname (Ah-Kee, Lam, Wong). Franco-Mauritians have French names. English is the language of law and government, but everyone speaks Kreol and most use French too; Bhojpuri is still heard in villages and among older people.",
